# NOTE: this bot also relays emoji reactions, so beyond the mood-bot scopes
# your Slack app needs the "reactions:read" bot scope and a subscription to
# the reaction_added event (in addition to message.im).
#
# NOTE: both /start and /matcha-party are real Slack slash commands, not
# plain-text messages (Slack intercepts anything starting with "/" as a
# command attempt). In your Slack app config: Features → Slash Commands →
# Create New Command → add both "/start" and "/matcha-party". Then add the
# "commands" bot scope under OAuth & Permissions, and reinstall the app.
#
# NOTE: to forward photos/videos/files (not just describe them as text), the
# bot also needs the "files:read" bot scope, and re-uploads them with
# files_upload_v2, which needs "files:write". Add both, then reinstall.
#
# NOTE: the Matcha Party feature also needs:
#   - "users:read" bot scope, so the bot can look up invitees' display names
#     (used via the users_info API call).
#   - "Interactivity & Shortcuts" turned ON in your Slack app config
#     (Features → Interactivity & Shortcuts). Since the bot runs in Socket
#     Mode, no Request URL is needed — just flip the toggle on. This is what
#     makes the spot-decision buttons, vote buttons, and order modal work.
# Reinstall the app to your workspace after adding the users:read scope.

# ── Slack Tokens ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bot User OAuth Token  (OAuth & Permissions → Bot User OAuth Token)
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-your-bot-token-here

# App-Level Token  (Basic Information → App-Level Tokens, scope: connections:write)
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-your-app-level-token-here

# ── Users ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your Slack user ID — the bot forwards everything the teammate below sends
# (messages AND emoji reactions) straight to you.
# Find it: click your name in Slack → View full profile → More → Copy member ID
ADMIN_USER_ID=U000000000

# The teammate's Slack user ID — this is who gets DMed the "bot details"
# setup questionnaire (e.g. Nouf). Every reply and reaction they give is
# relayed to ADMIN_USER_ID above.
TEAM_MEMBER_USER_ID=U000000000

# ── Matcha Party ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No extra config needed here — anyone in the workspace can start a party by
# running /matcha-party in their own DM with the bot, then @-mentioning
# whoever they want to invite. Whoever runs it becomes that party's "host".
# Invitees get asked to accept or decline before they're counted in. Multiple
# parties can run at once, but nobody (host or invitee) can be tied up in
# more than one at a time — if someone's already mid-party, they'll need to
# finish or decline there first. Everything else (RSVPs, spot decision,
# suggestions, votes, orders) happens over DMs the bot opens automatically.

# ── Test mode ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 to try a Matcha Party on your own: you can pick yourself as an
# invitee (playing host and invitee), and the onboarding relay to/from the
# teammate is switched off. For a quick run, set every step to 1 minute on
# the invite form.
# Leave unset (or 0) for real use.
# MATCHA_TEST_MODE=1

# ── Data folder ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where party_state.json and matcha_config.json are saved. Leave unset to keep
# them next to bot.py. On Northflank, attach a volume (e.g. mounted at /data)
# and set this to that path, so parties survive restarts and redeploys.
# MATCHA_STATE_DIR=/data

# ── Who can start a party ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated Slack user IDs. If set, only these people can run
# /matcha-party (anyone can still be invited). Leave unset to let everyone.
# MATCHA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=U000000000,U000000001
